<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hilarion of Kiev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 1037–1054</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Metropolitan Hilarion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hilarion was the first metropolitan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> of native Rus' origin, appointed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> in 1051 without waiting for the patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Before that he was a priest at the prince's church at Berestovo near Kiev. He is the author of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sermon-on-law-and-grace">Sermon on Law and Grace</a>, a founding work of East Slavic literature. He disappears from the sources soon after Yaroslav's death in 1054.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hilarion-of-kiev.jpg" alt="Hilarion of Kiev" width="300" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Illarion_ikona.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
